<!-- @before 2. The BFF Implementation :: guide -->
### Failure fallback does not create a timeout

The preserved `optionalUpstream` helper classifies selected failures and rethrows cancellation. It does not itself impose a deadline. A bounded optional policy needs an explicit owner and timeout boundary; cancelling the parent request must still cancel the request rather than produce healthy-looking content. [BFF ownership and tradeoffs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/backends-for-frontends).

<!-- @before 4. Failure Modes & Tradeoffs :: staff -->
### Keep the domain authority upstream

Give the mobile response a compatibility and latency owner, while pricing, permissions and inventory validation remain with their domain services. A smaller client call graph adds a deployed service and its operational burden.

<!-- @before 1. The Mobile Orchestration Problem :: visual -->
### The BFF owns the screen contract

```mermaid
flowchart TB
 m["Mobile client Supported screen contract"]
 b["Mobile BFF Orchestrate and shape"]
 r["Required domain Authoritative core data"]
 o["Optional dependency Bounded enrichment"]
 m -->|"one screen request"| b
 b -->|"required contract"| r
 b -->|"optional deadline"| o
 b -->|"stable response or error"| m
```

The BFF shapes required and optional dependency outcomes for one supported mobile contract. It does not become the authority for every upstream business rule.
